  "slug": "learning-reconstruction-galaxy-a30c",
  "summary": "Five preprints refine redshift-space models, field-level likelihoods, lensing reconstruction and 21-cm bispectrum probes for extracting more from observables.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is extracting underlying field information from observables through improved statistical modelling rather than compression alone. Redshift-space work includes a tracer-centred density-split clustering model with derived bias, and a result that parity-odd redshift-space distortion contributions to the isotropic galaxy 4PCF vanish after line-of-sight averaging. A faster field-level likelihood for large-scale galaxy surveys forecasts sigma(fNL)=4.1. In weak lensing, roulette amplitudes beyond flexion improve least-squares lens-mass reconstruction for clusters. For reionization, the phase gradient spectrum targets the squeezed 21-cm bispectrum, with SKA-Low forecasts. What is new is the emphasis on analytic or likelihood-based higher-order information, not simulation-trained inference as in the earlier grouping.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
